WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.292
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.592 --> 00:00:08.653
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:18.154
Unit thirty: Heritage of the Khmer World. In this unit, you will discuss Angkorian civilisation with careful, nuanced claims.

4
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:20.813
Khmer Empire legacy.

5
00:00:21.513 --> 00:00:27.796
Khmer Empire legacy means the lasting influence of the Khmer Empire on culture and identity.

6
00:00:28.096 --> 00:00:31.652
The Khmer Empire legacy can be seen from Thailand to Laos.

7
00:00:32.352 --> 00:00:35.933
Your turn. Say: Khmer Empire legacy.

8
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:40.412
Khmer Empire legacy.

9
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:43.172
Angkorian civilisation.

10
00:00:43.872 --> 00:00:50.493
Angkorian civilisation means the culture of the Khmer state centred on Angkor, from the 9th to 15th centuries.

11
00:00:50.793 --> 00:00:55.463
Angkorian civilisation produced astonishing temples and reservoirs.

12
00:00:56.163 --> 00:00:59.895
Your turn. Say: Angkorian civilisation.

13
00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:04.234
Angkorian civilisation.

14
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.576
hydraulic city.

15
00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:12.875
hydraulic city means a city organised around large systems for storing and moving water.

16
00:01:13.175 --> 00:01:16.463
Some scholars describe Angkor as a hydraulic city.

17
00:01:17.163 --> 00:01:20.442
Your turn. Say: hydraulic city.

18
00:01:23.142 --> 00:01:24.503
hydraulic city.

19
00:01:25.517 --> 00:01:27.156
Jayavarman VII.

20
00:01:27.856 --> 00:01:34.825
Jayavarman VII means the Buddhist Khmer king who built Angkor Thom, hospitals and rest houses.

21
00:01:35.125 --> 00:01:38.924
Jayavarman VII may have built over a hundred hospitals.

22
00:01:39.624 --> 00:01:43.263
Your turn. Say: Jayavarman VII.

23
00:01:45.963 --> 00:01:47.661
Jayavarman VII.

24
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:49.535
Bayon.

25
00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:55.799
Bayon means the temple at the centre of Angkor Thom, famous for its giant stone faces.

26
00:01:56.099 --> 00:01:59.213
The smiling faces of the Bayon look in four directions.

27
00:01:59.913 --> 00:02:01.857
Your turn. Say: Bayon.

28
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:05.349
Bayon.

29
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:07.398
devaraja.

30
00:02:08.098 --> 00:02:12.791
devaraja means the 'god-king' cult linking Khmer kings with the divine.

31
00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:18.178
Jayavarman II is said to have established the devaraja cult in 802.

32
00:02:18.878 --> 00:02:21.716
Your turn. Say: devaraja.

33
00:02:24.416 --> 00:02:25.441
devaraja.

34
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.590
Khmer script.

35
00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:33.877
Khmer script means the writing system of the Khmer language, developed from South Indian scripts.

36
00:02:34.177 --> 00:02:37.512
Khmer script has been used for more than fourteen centuries.

37
00:02:38.212 --> 00:02:41.166
Your turn. Say: Khmer script.

38
00:02:43.866 --> 00:02:44.914
Khmer script.

39
00:02:45.917 --> 00:02:47.672
Sanskrit inscription.

40
00:02:48.372 --> 00:02:54.935
Sanskrit inscription means a text carved in the classical Indian language Sanskrit.

41
00:02:55.235 --> 00:02:59.661
A Sanskrit inscription praises the king in elegant poetry.

42
00:03:00.361 --> 00:03:03.872
Your turn. Say: Sanskrit inscription.

43
00:03:06.572 --> 00:03:08.537
Sanskrit inscription.

44
00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.863
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:12.163 --> 00:03:15.486
Rithy, how do historians know so much about Angkor?

46
00:03:16.783 --> 00:03:23.302
Mostly from stone. Sanskrit inscriptions praised the kings, and Khmer script recorded daily life.

47
00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:29.614
Some say Angkor collapsed because its water system failed. Is that right?

48
00:03:30.917 --> 00:03:35.134
It may well have been one factor, but it can't have been the only one.

49
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:41.645
Interesting. So the hydraulic city theory is only part of the story?

50
00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.634
Arguably. Droughts, floods and shifting trade must have played a role too.

51
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.170
And Jayavarman VII? Was he really so exceptional?

52
00:03:54.483 --> 00:04:00.001
He might be the most ambitious builder in our history: the Bayon alone is extraordinary.

53
00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:05.452
Grammar focus: Modality and nuance (extended).

54
00:04:05.752 --> 00:04:08.344
Past modals show degrees of certainty.

55
00:04:08.644 --> 00:04:12.931
It may well have been one factor, but it can't have been the only one.

56
00:04:13.231 --> 00:04:16.206
Droughts and shifting trade must have played a role too.

57
00:04:16.506 --> 00:04:22.186
Historians rarely say 'it was'; past modals let you show exactly how certain the evidence is.

58
00:04:23.183 --> 00:04:26.381
Now read and listen. Reading the Stones of Angkor.

59
00:04:26.681 --> 00:04:32.919
Between the ninth and fifteenth centuries, Angkorian civilisation dominated much of mainland Southeast Asia.

60
00:04:34.217 --> 00:04:40.164
Its kings were linked with the divine through the devaraja cult, said to have begun in 802.

61
00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:51.948
Our knowledge comes largely from inscriptions: Sanskrit inscriptions in courtly verse, and texts in Khmer script recording land and temple duties.

62
00:04:53.250 --> 00:05:01.101
Archaeologists have also revealed that Angkor was a vast hydraulic city, with reservoirs, canals and embankments.

63
00:05:02.417 --> 00:05:09.107
Jayavarman VII, a Buddhist king, built Angkor Thom and the Bayon with its serene stone faces.

64
00:05:10.417 --> 00:05:16.526
Why Angkor declined remains debated; failures in the water system may well have contributed.

65
00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:23.473
However, war, climate extremes and a shift towards maritime trade must also have mattered.

66
00:05:24.783 --> 00:05:30.405
Whatever the cause, the Khmer Empire legacy remains central to Cambodian identity today.

67
00:05:31.717 --> 00:05:34.404
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:34.704 --> 00:05:37.481
The 'god-king' cult of the Khmer kings is the blank.

69
00:05:37.781 --> 00:05:41.969
A, devaraja. B, Bayon. C, Khmer script.

70
00:05:45.983 --> 00:05:48.726
The answer is A: devaraja.

71
00:05:50.217 --> 00:05:55.177
'It blank been the only cause' means it is impossible that it was the only cause.

72
00:05:55.477 --> 00:05:59.061
A, can't have. B, must have. C, may have.

73
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.920
The answer is A: can't have.

74
00:06:07.417 --> 00:06:10.066
'Trade must have played a role' expresses blank.

75
00:06:10.366 --> 00:06:16.702
A, a confident deduction. B, an impossibility. C, a weak guess.

76
00:06:20.717 --> 00:06:23.494
The answer is A: a confident deduction.

77
00:06:24.983 --> 00:06:28.852
A city organised around large water systems is a blank.

78
00:06:29.152 --> 00:06:36.045
A, hydraulic city. B, Sanskrit inscription. C, devaraja.

79
00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:43.106
The answer is A: hydraulic city.

80
00:06:44.617 --> 00:06:48.346
According to the text, who built Angkor Thom and the Bayon?

81
00:06:48.646 --> 00:06:56.607
A, Jayavarman VII. B, Jayavarman II. C, a French archaeologist.

82
00:07:00.617 --> 00:07:03.847
The answer is A: Jayavarman VII.

83
00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:10.951
Now it's your turn. Part one: Why do you think the Angkorian civilisation declined?

84
00:07:16.251 --> 00:07:21.165
Part two: Use three different past modals to show different degrees of certainty.

85
00:07:27.465 --> 00:07:37.945
The water system may well have failed after long droughts and floods. However, it can't have been the only reason. Shifting trade routes and wars must have weakened Angkor too.

86
00:07:38.245 --> 00:07:41.185
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:42.183 --> 00:07:48.679
Great work today! You learned eight new words and modality and nuance (extended).

88
00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:53.361
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:53.661 --> 00:07:56.452
See you in Unit thirty-one. Goodbye!

90
00:07:56.752 --> 00:07:59.277
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

